

. Web Applications

**Attack surface components**

* Login pages
* Input forms
* File uploads
* Cookies & sessions
* APIs & backend services

**Common attacks**

* SQL Injection
* Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
* Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)
* Authentication bypass
* Broken access control

**Example**

> An attacker injects malicious SQL through a login form to access the database.

---

. APIs (Very High-Risk Surface)

**Attack surface components**

* REST / GraphQL endpoints
* Authentication tokens
* Rate limits
* API versions

**Common attacks**

* Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA)
* Token theft
* API abuse
* Mass assignment

**Example**

> An attacker changes a user ID in an API request to access another user’s data.

---

. Network Infrastructure

**Attack surface components**

* Open ports
* Firewalls
* VPNs
* DNS servers

**Common attacks**

* Port scanning
* DDoS
* Man-in-the-Middle (MITM)
* Exploiting unpatched services

**Example**

> An exposed RDP port allows brute-force access.

---

. Endpoints (User Devices)

**Attack surface components**

* Laptops, mobiles, IoT
* USB ports
* Installed software
* OS vulnerabilities

**Common attacks**

* Malware
* Ransomware
* Keylogging
* Privilege escalation

**Example**

> A phishing email installs malware on a user’s laptop.

---

. Cloud Environments

**Attack surface components**

* Public storage buckets
* IAM roles
* Management consoles
* Serverless functions

**Common attacks**

* Misconfigured storage exposure
* Credential theft
* Privilege escalation
* API abuse

**Example**

> A public S3 bucket leaks customer data.

---

. Identity & Access Management (IAM)

**Attack surface components**

* Passwords
* MFA implementations
* Single Sign-On (SSO)
* Privileged accounts

**Common attacks**

* Credential stuffing
* Phishing
* MFA fatigue attacks

**Example**

> Reused passwords allow attackers to log in from breached credentials.

---

. Software Supply Chain

**Attack surface components**

* Third-party libraries
* CI/CD pipelines
* Update mechanisms

**Common attacks**

* Dependency poisoning
* Malicious updates
* Compromised build systems

**Example**

> A vulnerable open-source library gives attackers system access.

---

. Data & Storage

**Attack surface components**

* Databases
* Backups
* Logs
* Secrets

**Common attacks**

* Data exfiltration
* Unauthorized access
* Misconfigurations

**Example**

> A backup server is publicly accessible without authentication.


. Human Attack Surface

**Attack surface components**

* Emails
* Social media
* Help desks
* Physical access

**Common attacks**

* Phishing
* Social engineering
* Insider threats

**Example**

> An attacker impersonates IT support to reset a password.

---

. Physical Infrastructure

**Attack surface components**

* Servers
* USB access
* Network closets

**Common attacks**

* Hardware tampering
* Rogue devices

**Example**

> An attacker plugs a malicious USB into an office computer.



